# Vulnerability details

# Bug Description

The recent code update introduces the functionality for authorities to reduce the total voting power by invoking the `decreaseTotalVotingPower()` function of the party. However, this functionality can lead to issues when used in the time frame after a `ReraiseETHCrowdfund` is finalized, but users have not yet claimed their voting power. The problem arises if the total voting power gets decreased after being increased during the finalization of the `reraiseCrowdfund`, and users attempt to claim voting power for one of their existing NFTs. To claim their voting power the users will call the `claim()` function which will then subsequently call to `increaseVotingPower()`. In such cases, the capping of `mintedVotingPower` to `totalVotingPower` is utilized, leading to users receiving less voting power than they paid for.

The issue is rooted in the check within the `increaseVotingPower()` function:
```solidity
// Cap voting power to remaining unminted voting power supply. Allow
// minting past total voting power if minting party cards for initial
// crowdfund when there is no total voting power.
if (totalVotingPower != 0 && totalVotingPower - mintedVotingPower_ < votingPower) {
	unchecked {
		votingPower = totalVotingPower - mintedVotingPower_;
	}
}
```

In this case the user will only receive the difference between `totalVotingPower` and `mintedVotingPower` instead of the actual voting power he payed for. This can be very close to the amount that the user paid for, or in worst case 0 if the `totalVotingPower`got reduced to the `mintedVotingPower.

