# Vulnerability details


## Impact
The voting power parameters and calculation allows for a situation where `execute()` will revert even when a proposal is Ready to execute.

## Proof of Concept
In PartyGovernance.sol
```solidity
function _isUnanimousVotes(
    uint96 totalVotes,
    uint96 totalVotingPower
) private pure returns (bool) {
    uint256 acceptanceRatio = (totalVotes * 1e4) / totalVotingPower;
    // If >= 99.99% acceptance, consider it unanimous.
    // The minting formula for voting power is a bit lossy, so we check
    // for slightly less than 100%.
    return acceptanceRatio >= 0.9999e4;
}
```
This reverts if `totalVotes > type(uint96).max / 1e4`. In this situation `_getProposalFlags()`, which always calls `_isUnanimousVotes()`, reverts. `_getProposalFlags()` is always called by `execute()` which therefore reverts. 

`_isUnanimousVotes()` is also called in `_getProposalStatus()` when votes are passed but we are still within the execution delay. The consequence here is that a unanimous vote does not return Ready immediately but instead reverts, so that one has to wait for the execution delay even when the vote is unanimous. This DoS is transient and less severe, but because of the above it will revert in `execute()` later anyway.

This might easily occur for example if 100% of the voting power is set to be one ray, i.e. 1e27, which seems a considerable risk considering the widespread use of this unit.

